st supervised  learning on a curated data set of expert  state action pairs  but the issue with imitation learning is  that you're given this data and you  basically copy it exactly so we know  that imitation learning performs poorly  if the data is going to come from  imperfect experts because you're  indiscriminately copying all of the  actions in the data set and so here in  this figure you see a comparison between  imitation learning methods and offline  rl methods which are able to  find a better policy on top of the  imitation data  and the other issue with imitation  learning is the resulting policies are  brittle and they don't generalize well  so if you go off of the support of the  state action data set that you had to  copy your policy doesn't know what to do  whereas reinforcement learning can  explore beyond that and potentially  improve and i'll show the brittleness of  imitation learning and some results that  i'm about to present  so when i'm talking about social  learning with multi-agent rl i'm talking  about something a little bit different  here i'm thinking of you're in a  naturalistic setting where there are  other agents present in the environment  but they're not necessarily motivated to  teach you so imagine real world settings  like the human world there are a bunch  of other agents running around but  they're just going about their business  they're not trying to actually teach you  any information or provide you a curated  data set of what you should do so this  is true of settings like autonomous  driving there are other cars on the road  and some of them are  really intelligent and might be doing  the right thing but some of them  actually might be bad drivers so you  don't want to indiscriminately copy  everyone  what you actually want to do is have a  social learning ability to determine who  is a good model that you should be  learning from and in what context  and so basically social learning is the  skill of learning how to acquire