ng at all so they're much dumber  than this little one-inch fish we talked  about so in this work we just did a  simple fix to address this problem we  said in addition to our agent deciding  on its rl policy it's just going to have  an auxiliary prediction loss to predict  its own next observation  and what's interesting is that implicit  in being able to accurately predict the  next observation you have to predict  what the other agent is going to do and  because you need to predict where they  are in the frame  so this was enough that it forced the  representation of the agent to encode  something about what the expert was  doing  and what we saw is that this simple fix  actually allowed our agents to  successfully learn socially from the  experts so that's that green line you're  seeing here  um they've now just with this auxiliary  prediction loss  reached the performance of imitation  learning agents which were given direct  access to the state action pairs of  other agents whereas our agents don't  have that they're just in the  environment with the expert  and so this is kind of cool you can say  you can learn effectively from other  agents in your environment even if you  don't have access to their data  but you might say i might have access to  their data like in autonomous driving i  probably do have a data set of the state  action pairs of a bunch of drivers so  why is it worth it to do this social  learning approach rather than just doing  imitation learning  well let me show you this so  what we're seeing here is the  generalization performance of both  imitation learners rl agents and social  learners to a series of new environments  that they've never seen before  and so what you're seeing is in the new  environment this green line is  an expert that's been trained in that  new environment so in each new  environment there are experts running  around that know how to do the task  the original rl agents in orange  generalize poorly to the new  environ