h  another student named angelo spilos  who's a great also and um here we're  going to use successor features to help  with social learning so if you're not  familiar with success or features i'll  just give you a quick rundown as you  know in rl we're trying to you know  estimate our total expected future  reward um which we can parametrize as a  q value  um which is the reward expected from  being in a particular state and taking a  particular action  successor features decompose this into  two components a psi component and a w  component and so what psi is doing is  essentially s doing all the hard work  that we have to do in rl of estimating  what's going to happen in the future so  psi is going to estimate  over the course of an episode how much  of these state features phi we're going  to accumulate  so imagine we were in a grid world and  we just had to pick up different types  of fruits so apples versus bananas you  can imagine phi would say at a given  time set did i pick up an apple or a  banana or nothing right so it's just a  bit vector  psy would say over the course of an  entire episode how many apples and  bananas am i going to pick up following  my policy right  and then w  is just a preference vector so this  stays the same across every episode and  it just describes how to map those state  features to reward so if the agent  prefers bananas twice as much as apples  we can just take that 2 1 preference  vector and multiply it with our you know  psi and compute our q value which is our  total expected reward from what what's  going to happen in the future  so why is this important to do well the  nice thing about successor features is  it lets you adapt very quickly to new  preferences right so if at test time i  say look you learned to pick up twice as  many bananas as apples but now i want to  change your preferences to pick up twice  as many apples as bananas so i'm  changing w to one two  i can instantly compute  which action will lead to the highest  payoff under that new reward using my uh  existing sai so i can do a very quick  adaptation to new preferences so this  actually helps a lot with generalization  so that's nice  so what we wanted to do is apply the  same concept to learning from other  agents  so what we're going to do is we're going  to model ourselves the ego agent as well  as all of the other agents in our  driving environment all the other agents  that are present on the road  using the successor features approach so  every agent is going to have this  estimated sigh that's estimating what  state features is going to encounter  according to its policy and this w which  are essentially its preferences  so we can learn the ego agent's  successor features or successful  representation just with rl as normal  and for the other agents we can model  them with maximum entropy irl so  essentially we say the other agents are  soft optimal we observe the probability  of the actions that they're taking and  we assume that an  action is exponentially more probable if  it has a higher q value or if the  successor equation is higher  and so we can use this to infer the  successor representation of the other  agents  but what's cool about that is then we  can say look  i inferred the other agents preferences  but i actually don't care about their  preferences i only care about my own  preferences  so what i can do is  substitute my preferences for every  other agent's preferences  and then ask myself  using this generalized policy  improvement idea  could i get a higher reward if i just  acted like one of the other agents  according to my own preferences  so basically at each time step i say  look if i were to take the action i'm  going to take my preferences and combine  it with agent 2's psi and that's gonna  give me the recommended action that um i  agent two would take if it were  optimizing my preferences and if i  compute that that action has a higher  payoff than my own action  then i can selectively imitate the other  agents to the degree that i compute it  would pay off for my reward function  so that's pretty fun  but of course in order to get this to  work we have to choose a phi in a very  specific way  so you might remember if we look at this  equation for psi and phi  psi is policy specific so it's counting  up what's going to happen in the future  if i'm acting like a particular policy  like agent two  but  phi is actually policy agnostic right  it's just the features that are  happening at a single state  so what we did in this work is we say  look all of the agents can share the  same underlying feature representation  phi  and  that's going to allow us to swap our  preferences with other agents  preferences we can ensure that those are  compatible  and  we can build up every agent's side using  the same phi  and so we get a lot of data as we're  trying to model all the agents in the  environment we get a lot of data that  can help us learn that phi  and whenever we learn something about  one agent that actually improves our  modeling of the other agent through  improving this feature representation  phi  and we learn these  phi's and size using this inverse  temporal difference loss this basically  looks like a bellman error  but without rewards  and um we can also improve our phi with  rewards when we're acting as the ego  agent so as ourself the ego agent we can  interact with the environment and get  rewards we can use that to further  refine our fi so we basically get a lot  of signal about how to train this  feature representation you can think of  it extracting a sort of a universal  feature representation that's capable of  describing any agent's behavior it's  extracting all the features relevant to  describing any agent's behavior  and so to zoom out for a second from the  math and give you the higher picture  basically what we're doing here is  um we're creating this feedback loop  where the more you learn from others you  can selectively imitate their actions  and choose actions that will lead to a  higher payoff for you based on copying  other agents when they're relevant  and then the more you act in the  environment and gain experience with the  environment and observe rewards you can  further refine that shared feature  representation which improves your  modeling of others  so the more you interact the more you  model others the better model the better  you model others the more you can steal  their  their actions and and get better reward  and so what's nice about this is we  tested it in a variety of environments  so some autonomous driving environments  and a grid world environment and a more  visually high dimensional environment  called fruitbot  and what we see is pretty interesting  so in the case where all the other  agents are relevant to what your task is  so essentially all the other agents are  doing the exact same task as you then  the best thing you can do is imitation  learning  that's going to learn the fastest but  our method learns pretty quickly it  learns more quickly than rl methods  in the case where the other agents on  the road are doing something exactly  wrong like parking on the highway if you  imitate them you'll do catastrophically  poorly so you see imitation fails in  this case the rl methods still do well  and so does our method  but in the realistic case where you're  in a multi-agent environment where some  of the agents are doing something  relevant but many of the others are  doing something  irrelevant then our method outperforms  both imitation learning and rl um  because it gets the best of both worlds  and the nice thing is we get some side  benefits of this method so  as uh  as part of doing our method we're  implicitly modeling  uh what other agents actions are going  to be next so we're being able to  predict other agents actions and we see  that versus different imitation learning  methods  our method can more effectively predict  what the other agents are going to do on  a held out test set especially when the  other agent's task changes  and our method works as an effective  inverse rl method so we can infer the  reward function that other agents are  optimizing and then effectively optimize  it ourself  and finally our method improves  generalization so  as opposed to imitation learning where  it can take up to a hundred new episodes  to adapt to  successfully imitating an agent in a new  task our agent can adapt in one shot so  this is actually pretty cool because  let's say during training you've only  ever been asked to pick up bananas in  this you know fruit picking world  at test time but you've observed another  agent picking up apples  at test time our method is able to adapt  one shot to the apple picking task  because they've already observed a  policy that's good at picking apples and  they can just switch to it by adjusting  their preference vector w so we get a  little bit closer to this one-inch fish  in terms of generalization  so in conclusion social learning is a  powerful mechanism that can help rl  agents rapidly adapt to new environments  and learn complex behavior  all right so let's go to the last part  of the talk so in this part of the talk  i want to talk about learning from human  ai interaction or learning from  interactions with humans  so  i think i'll start with this obvious  example about why you might want to do  this so  this is a real interaction someone had  with their alexa they say alexa what's  the right way to walmart and alexa says  the right way to spell walmart is  w-a-l-blah blah blah so this is  obviously not what the user wanted and  i'm going to bet you that the next thing  that they say contains some hint that  they were frustrated whether it's in  their tone of voice or their facial  expression there's some clue there that  alexa could learn not to do this again  and yet the way we often train ai  systems is still focused on using these  types of manual button