r fun we've seen  this  it can minimize repetition which is  important to maintaining conversation  quality as we saw and it can do this  more effectively again at the sentence  level than the word level so in this  conversation you know the user says how  are you i'm a reservoir engineer cool i  am still in college today is my first  day of vacation it's nice  it can also optimize the semantic  similarities so staying on topic with  the user so in this conversation the  user mentions ed sheeran the bot says  what genre is it and then the bot asks  for any recommendations so it's staying  on topic with what the user is saying  and finally and i think most importantly  in this work we also discuss the idea of  minimizing the toxicity of the model so  whenever you're training these dialogue  models on large-scale internet data sets  we know that they can exhibit biases  they can be exhibit racist and sexist  language  so what we did was we trained a detector  a classifier that says is this language  toxic and then what we can do is use  that as a reward function for our rl  model to try to minimize that toxicity  level and we see that our model is able  to do this pretty effectively and we  have  some results of that in the paper i  think this is actually really important  to emphasize why you might want to do rl  on top of language models right there  might be some non-differentiable metrics  that are really important for you to  minimize like toxicity that rl gives you  the ability to do that so whatever  metric you can decide should be a  quality of your text you can use these  rl methods to optimize for that  all right so in conclusion this is the  first work to propose kale control from  a pre-trained prior as a way to handle  offline or batch rl but the key thing  here is that we're using reinforcement  learning to to improve based on human  feedback and we showed that learning  from implicit signals from a human like  sentiment scales better than learning  from