ze  effectively to new environments by  looking at where other fish are  gathering to find a safe source of food  and in the uk there's this fun example  of birds that learn to open milktop  bottles not because each bird  independently discovered this behavior  but because they could learn by  observing other birds  and of course many authors have argued  that the key differentiator that humans  have versus other animals is actually  our social learning abilities and it's  those incredible social learning  abilities that have led to our  impressive cognitive cultural and  technological development  so social learning can accelerate  learning individual learning can be  unsafe error-prone and time-consuming  but social learning lets you stand on  the shoulders of giants  and so my  research works on social reinforcement  learning in two contexts one in  multi-agent interactions where you're  learning from other ai agents and two in  human ai interaction where you're  learning from humans and i'm going to  talk about both of these aspects of my  work today  and to give you a broader overview of my  work i've worked on  the more human ai side where i've worked  on detecting human social and emotional  cues  then learning from those cues and  interactions with humans  teaching multiple agents to coordinate  with each other  multi-agent social learning specifically  and uh setting up multi-agent systems  such that they give rise to the  emergence of complex behavior  and so for today's talk i'm going to  talk about those two aspects  and i'll start with this social learning  aspect first because i think it kind of  gives a lot of context what i mean when  i say social learning  so is social learning just imitation  learning i mean i said it helps you  stand on the shoulders of giants but so  does imitation learning if you want to  quickly acquire behavior from something  an expert already knows how to do you  could just use imitation learning so  imitation learning is just supervised  learning on a curated data set of expert  state action pairs  but the issue with imitation learning is  that you're given this data and you  basically copy it exactly so we know  that imitation learning performs poorly  if the data is going to come from  imperfect experts because you're  indiscriminately copying all of the  actions in the data set and so here in  this figure you see a comparison between  imitation learning methods and offline  rl methods which are able to  find a better policy on top of the  imitation data  and the other issue with imitation  learning is the resulting policies are  brittle and they don't generalize well  so if you go off of the support of the  state action data set that you had to  copy your policy doesn't know what to do  whereas reinforcement learning can  explore beyond that and potentially  improve and i'll show the brittleness of  imitation learning and some results that  i'm about to present  so when i'm talking about social  learning with multi-agent rl i'm talking  about something a little bit different  here i'm thinking of you're in a  naturalistic setting where there are  other agents present in the environment  but they're not necessarily motivated to  teach you so imagine real world settings  like the human world there are a bunch  of other agents running around but  they're just going about their business  they're not trying to actually teach you  any information or provide you a curated  data set of what you should do so this  is true of settings like autonomous  driving there are other cars on the road  and some of them are  really intelligent and might be doing  the right thing but some of them  actually might be bad drivers so you  don't want to indiscriminately copy  everyone  what you actually want to do is have a  social learning ability to determine who  is a good model that you should be  learning from and in what context  and so basically social learning is the  skill of learning how to acquire