ng at all so they're much dumber  than this little one-inch fish we talked  about so in this work we just did a  simple fix to address this problem we  said in addition to our agent deciding  on its rl policy it's just going to have  an auxiliary prediction loss to predict  its own next observation  and what's interesting is that implicit  in being able to accurately predict the  next observation you have to predict  what the other agent is going to do and  because you need to predict where they  are in the frame  so this was enough that it forced the  representation of the agent to encode  something about what the expert was  doing  and what we saw is that this simple fix  actually allowed our agents to  successfully learn socially from the  experts so that's that green line you're  seeing here  um they've now just with this auxiliary  prediction loss  reached the performance of imitation  learning agents which were given direct  access to the state action pairs of  other agents whereas our agents don't  have that they're just in the  environment with the expert  and so this is kind of cool you can say  you can learn effectively from other  agents in your environment even if you  don't have access to their data  but you might say i might have access to  their data like in autonomous driving i  probably do have a data set of the state  action pairs of a bunch of drivers so  why is it worth it to do this social  learning approach rather than just doing  imitation learning  well let me show you this so  what we're seeing here is the  generalization performance of both  imitation learners rl agents and social  learners to a series of new environments  that they've never seen before  and so what you're seeing is in the new  environment this green line is  an expert that's been trained in that  new environment so in each new  environment there are experts running  around that know how to do the task  the original rl agents in orange  generalize poorly to the new  environments we know rl can be kind of  brittle in that way  um  so do the imitation learners they're  extremely brittle much more brittle than  rl  but the social learners are shown in  purple here so they actually perform  pretty well across all three  environments and why this is happening  is they basically learned a policy about  how to acquire information from other  agents that they can deploy in a new  environment to figure out what they're  supposed to be doing  so i think this is pretty interesting  there's also another um interesting  finding i just want to highlight that we  can show that  social learning enables the agents to  discover skills that they would never  have discovered if they trained alone  since the beginning  so what you're seeing here is the  performance of solo learners as the line  on the cross the bottom  um and then agents that were trained  with either only social learning or a  mix of solo and social episodes so in  the solo episodes the agent is alone and  the social episodes are with the experts  and what you see is that  as you uh use social learning basically  the agents start to discover a behavior  from learning with the experts that  they're never able to discover if they  train alone but if you continue using  only social learning then the agents  become dependent on the presence of the  experts and their performance in the  solo environment um decays  uh so it actually gets worse but if you  do this mix of solo and social training  we see that the uh agents trained in  that mix  preserve their performance in the solo  environment  using the information that they gain  from training with experts so  essentially they're acquiring a skill  from the experts which they are able to  maintain and deploy when they're alone  which is fine  but this method was a pretty simple way  of helping the agents to engage in  social learning so can we develop more  sophisticated methods for doing social  learning  and so this was a project i did with  another student named angelo spilos  who's a great also and um here we're  going to use successor features to help  with social learning so if you're not  familiar with success or features i'll  just give you a quick rundown as you  know in rl we're trying to you know  estimate our total expected future  reward um which we can parametrize as a  q value  um which is the reward expected from  being in a particular state and taking a  particular action  successor features decompose this into  two components a psi component and a w  component and so what psi is doing is  essentially s doing all the hard work  that we have to do in rl of estimating  what's going to happen in the future so  psi is going to estimate  over the course of an episode how much  of these state features phi we're going  to accumulate  so imagine we were in a grid world and  we just had to pick up different types  of fruits so apples versus bananas you  can imagine phi would say at a given  time set did i pick up an apple or a  banana or nothing right so it's just a  bit vector  psy would say over the course of an  entire episode how many apples and  bananas am i going to pick up following  my policy right  and then w  is just a preference vector so this  stays the same across every episode and  it just describes how to map those state  features to reward so if the agent  prefers bananas twice as much as apples  we can just take that 2 1 preference  vector and multiply it with our you know  psi and compute our q value which is our  total expected reward from what what's  going to happen in the future  so why is this important to do well the  nice thing about successor features is  it lets you adapt very quickly to new  preferences right so if at test time i  say look you learned to pick up twice as  many bananas as apples but now i want to  change your preferences to pick up twice  as many apples as bananas so i'm  changing w to one two  i can instantly compute  which action will lead to the highest  payoff under that new reward using my uh  existing sai so i can do a very quick  adaptation to new preferences so this  actually helps a lot with generalization  so that's nice  so what we wanted to do is apply the  same concept to learning from other  agents  so what we're going to do is we're going  to model ourselves the ego agent as well  as all of the other agents in our  driving environment all the other agents  that are present on the road  using the successor features approach so  every agent is going to have this  estimated sigh that's estimating what  state features is going to encounter  according to its policy and this w which  are essentially its preferences  so we can learn the ego agent's  successor features or successful  representation just with rl as normal  and for the other agents we can model  them with maximum entropy irl so  essentially we say the other agents are  soft optimal we observe the probability  of the actions that they're taking and  we assume that an  action is exponentially more probable if  it has a higher q value or if the  successor equation is higher  and so we can use this to infer the  successor representation of the other  agents  but what's cool about that is then we  can say look  i inferred the other agents preferences  but i actually don't care about their  preferences i only care about my own  preferences  so what i can do is  substitute my preferences for every  other agent's preferences  and then ask myself  using this generalized policy  improvement idea  could i get a higher reward if i just  acted like one of the other agents  according to my own preferences  so basically at each time step i say  look if i were to take the action i'm  going to take my preferences and combine  it with agent 2's psi and that's gonna  give me the recommended action that um i  agent two would take if it were  optimizing my preferences and if i  compute that that action has a higher  payoff than my own action  then i can selectively imitate the other  agents to the degree that i compute it  would pay off for my reward function  so that's pretty fun  but of course in order to get this to  work we have to choose a phi in a very  specific way  so you might remember if we look at this  equation for psi and phi  psi is policy specific so it's counting  up what's going to happen in the future  if i'm acting like a particular policy  like agent two  but  phi is actually policy agnostic right  it's just the features that are  happening at a single state  so what we did in this work is we say  look all of the agents can share the  same underlying feature representation  phi  and  that's going to allow us to swap our  preferences with other agents  preferences we can ensure that those are  compatible  and  we can build up every agent's side using  the same phi  and so we get a lot of data as we're  trying to model all the agents in the  environment we get a lot of data that  can help us learn that phi  and whenever we learn something about  one agent that actually improves our  modeling of the other agent through  improving this feature representation  phi  and we learn these  phi's and size using this inverse  temporal difference loss this basically  looks like a bellman error  but without rewards  and um we can also improve our phi with  rewards when we're acting as the ego  agent so as ourself the ego agent we can  interact with the environment and get  rewards we can use that to further  refine our fi so we basically get a lot  of signal about how to train this  feature representation you can think of  it extracting a sort of a universal  feature representation that's capable of  describing any agent's behavior it's  extracting all the features relevant to  describing any agent's behavior  and so to zoom out for a second from the  math and give you the higher picture  basically what we're doing here is  um we're creating this feedback loop  where the more you learn from others you  can selectively imitate their actions  and choose actions that will lead to a  higher payoff for you based on copying  other agents when they're relevant  and then the more you act in the  environment and gain experience with the  environment and observe rewards you can  further refine that shared feature  representation which improves your  modeling of others  so the more you interact the more you  model others the better model the better  you model others the more you can steal  their  their actions and and get better reward  and so what's nice about this is we  tested it in a variety of environments  so some autonomous driving environments  and a grid world environment and a more  visually high dimensional environment  called fruitbot  and what we see is pretty interesting  so in the case where all the other  agents are relevant to what your task is  so essentially all the other agents are  doing the exact same task as you then  the best thing you can do is imitation  learning  that's going to learn the fastest but  our method learns pretty quickly it  learns more quickly than rl methods  in the case where the other agents on  the road are doing something exactly  wrong like parking on the highway if you  imitate them you'll do catastrophically  poorly so you see imitation fails in  this case the rl methods still do well  and so does our method  but in the realistic case where you're  in a multi-agent environment where some  of the agents are doing something  relevant but many of the others are  doing something  irrelevant then our method outperforms  both imitation learning and rl um  because it gets the best of both worlds  and the nice thing is we get some side  benefits of this method so  as uh  as part of doing our method we're  implicitly modeling  uh what other agents actions are going  to be next so we're being able to  predict other agents actions and we see  that versus different imitation learning  methods  our method can more effectively predict  what the other agents are going to do on  a held out test set especially when the  other agent's task changes  and our method works as an effective  inverse rl method so we can infer the  reward function that other agents are  optimizing and then effectively optimize  it ourself  and finally our method improves  generalization so  as opposed to imitation learning where  it can take up to a hundred new episodes  to adapt to  successfully imitating an agent in a new  task our agent can adapt in one shot so  this is actually pretty cool because  let's say during training you've only  ever been asked to pick up bananas in  this you know fruit picking world  at test time but you've observed another  agent picking up apples  at test time our method is able to adapt  one shot to the apple picking task  because they've already observed a  policy that's good at picking apples and  they can just switch to it by adjusting  their preference vector w so we get a  little bit closer to this one-inch fish  in terms of generalization  so in conclusion social learning is a  powerful mechanism that can help rl  agents rapidly adapt to new environments  and learn complex behavior  all right so let's go to the last part  of the talk so in this part of the talk  i want to talk about learning from human  ai interaction or learning from  interactions with humans  so  i think i'll start with this obvious  example about why you might want to do  this so  this is a real interaction someone had  with their alexa they say alexa what's  the right way to walmart and alexa says  the right way to spell walmart is  w-a-l-blah blah blah so this is  obviously not what the user wanted and  i'm going to bet you that the next thing  that they say contains some hint that  they were frustrated whether it's in  their tone of voice or their facial  expression there's some clue there that  alexa could learn not to do this again  and yet the way we often train ai  systems is still focused on using these  types of manual button presses for the  human to indicate whether they were  satisfied with the results  but if we were able to learn from this  type of social feedback like facial  expressions tone of voice um we could  learn much more ubiquitously and  naturally so people are giving us these  signals for free anyway as they're  interacting with ai systems so if we  could just pick up on them we could  learn more effectively  and so this is the focus of a project  that we worked on on learning from  implicit human feedback and dialogue  so what we did was we created a system  where we could deploy dialogue models to  the internet and have them interact with  people live  and here's an example of an interaction  and what you see is that when the user  or when the bot says something um that  doesn't make sense like sorry to hear  the user says that doesn't make sense  and we can actually detect from the text  itself that this is a good clue that the  bot is doing something wrong so for  example we can detect a negative  sentiment from that from that text and  that can serve as a negative reward we  can use to train the bot not to do this  again  similarly when the conversation is going  well  the user sentiment is positive  indicating that the bot is doing a good  job  so we can use this type of implicit  signal in the text itself to try to  train the bot to have better  conversations  and so essentially what we did was as i  mentioned created this online platform  for collecting this data  and  we did this by first pre-training a  bunch of dialogue models we had to  scrape some new data sets in order to do  this  and we put these models online and as  you can see in the interface we include  a few different features so we included  an upvote down vote button so the user  could manually indicate  whether they thought the bot was doing a  good or a bad job with that specific  utterance and we also included what  sentiment we were detecting from the  user so we showed that back to them to  say what the bot was thinking of of the  sentence that the person just set  and our hypothesis is that trying to  train the bot based on these explicit  manual upvotes isn't going to scale as  effectively as training on this implicit  sentiment from the user because while  the upvotes are the ground truth  the user's just not going to want to  provide them very often in order to do  that they have to deviate from the  natural chat interface that they would  normally be using and go out of their  way to provide that signal whereas the  sentiment is something that we get in  every piece of text regardless  um but in order to  do this work we actually want to dig a  little bit deeper and go beyond just  sentiment were there other implicit cues  that we could use from the text to  determine whether the conversation was  going well  so we scraped the literature on both  dialogue models and human human  conversations to look at what kinds of  signals are indicative of an empathetic  and engaging conversations and we came  up with a bunch of different options and  we looked at the relationship between  these metrics and the up builds and  downloads that we received in our  initial data collection and so we did  treat the uploads and downloads as  ground truth and we actually found that  these signals related reliably to them  so essentially um  asking questions turned out to be  related to getting upvotes  we found this from the human human  conversation literature it's supposed to  be an important active listening skill  reducing repetition on the part of the  bot is very good because that was from  the dialogue model literature and it  proved to be true  and finally we found that  really engaging with the user is  important so eliciting positive  sentiment from the user had a strong  relationship with um  with good conversations and eliciting  laughter and finally just staying on  topic with the user by maximizing the  similarity between the bot sentence and  the user sentence both in terms of the  sentence embedding and word similarity  so these were the metrics that we stuck  with for how to train a good  conversation model  and so basically what we wanted to do is  use reinforcement learning to actually  learn to optimize these metrics  by putting our bots on the web and  having them interact with humans so  basically instead of your normal rl  paradigm where the agent is interacting  with a simulated environment in our  paradigm the agent is actually  interacting with a human a human is the  environment that's providing rewards  based on whether they enjoyed the  conversation  um  but wait did i just say i wanted to put  an agent online and have it interact  with humans on the internet to teach it  how to talk  i think we've seen how badly that can go  in the past so  we decided in the context of this  project that we actually needed to  carefully test any model that we trained  offline before deploying it back to  human so we can't have it learning  online from interacting with humans  about how to talk  and so what this necessitated is  actually to do  uh  fully offline learning which means that  our normal interaction or exploration  part of the rl loop is broken um so  instead of being able to explore to find  which actions lead to high payoff we  have to learn entirely offline so we're  in this off policy from static data  batch or offline rl setting this is a  very challenging setting because a lot  of rl algorithms are built such that um  they're optimistic in the face of  uncertainty and the exploration is  actually key to refining the estimates  that they learn  so  i'll give you a quick illustration of  why offline rl is hard so let's say  you're this little robot and you're  trying to learn to walk to this red flag  but you're only given the data in a  single batch of data and the probability  that your c a state in the batch is  shown in yellow here so basically  anything in this region that's white off  of this yellow support is not in the  batch data  so the issue with offline rl is that if  at any point your policy is initialized  or  learns that going into this region of  unexplored space is a good idea it has a  high estimate for this region of space  it has no way to refine that estimate  because it doesn't have any data from  that region of space and  actually that bad estimate will  propagate backwards  into all of the preceding states right  because we're trying to estimate our  total expected future reward and so if  we ever think this is valuable that  incorrect estimate is going to be  propagated into all the preceding  estimates so basically estimating  value in offline rl can be arbitrarily  bad  but we saw this with a very simple thing  we just said let's add a kl constraint  so that we say that as the  agent is learning its rl policy it  should actually minimize the kl  divergence between its policy and the  support of the batch data so basically  search within this region of highly  rewarding behaviors here in order to  find the optimal policy so to put that  in math basically we're just doing kl  control from a pre-trained prior model  of probability of a given s  and this can be  learned from the batch data  or in the case of language we actually  have a really good model of which  actions are acceptable given the state  because this is just our pre-trained  language model  and so what we do  is we say instead of just maximizing our  expected reward as we would normally do  in rl we're going to minimize the kl  divergence between our rl policy and  this pre-trained model or our language  model  and so  in when we're estimating the q value  this amounts to adding two simple terms  we simply um subtract the log  probability of our policy so this is  equivalent to doing entropy  regularization which is a good idea to  do in dialogue anyway and we add the  probability under the prior um and so  this just says you're going to get a  reward if you take actions that look  probable according to the language model  and we do a few other tricks which you  can check out in the paper if you're  interested  but let me first establish that this is  an effective offline rl method so this  idea of kale control from a model of  what's probable in the batch is actually  performing well across a bunch of open  ai gym environments and outperforming a  batch constraint q learning which was  the competing offline rl method at the  time  but let's see this in the context of  dialogue so what happens if you don't do  this and you just try to use offline rl  on dialogue data  what you see is that your model starts  saying things like where did you say to  me you want to talk to you about you  so it's basically using very unrealistic  sentences so it doesn't know what is a  realistic sentence once it's off of the  support of the batch data so it can  become increasingly unrealistic and it's  just trying to maximize the rewards we  gave it by for example exploiting asking  questions because that's one of the  simplest rewards that it's able to do  that we gave it  um  instead of actually doing something more  sophisticated  but what we see is that when you add  kale control the model says things like  hey i hope you have a great day and i  wish you the best  so  through this constraint it's now learned  to use more realistic language but in  order to optimize these rewards for  example eliciting positive sentiment  it's learned to be very cheerful polite  and supportive so we inadvertently came  up with a metric that trains the bot to  be canadian  but what we see is that when we deploy  these models to new humans at test signs  so they're now back on the internet and  they're interacting with a new set of  humans we see that our models are able  to elicit significantly higher human  reward from the humans so they're able  to elicit more positive sentiment and  more laughter than  any of the baseline methods including  the original language model  so we see that we're able to effectively  learn with rl using this technique  um if we compare how optimizing each of  the different reward functions in  isolation leads to um  affects the human evaluation basically  we have a human evaluation where they're  raiding the bots on the quality fluency  diversity etc we're also looking at the  votes the upvotes down votes that each  bot received and the amount of human  reward they elicited  what we see is that as we suspected  um optimizing for  eliciting positive sentiment or  displaying positive sentiment leads to  the highest quality and the highest  human reward so we're seeing that affect  is important to good conversations which  was nice because this is keeping my  advisor of the affective computing group  very happy  but in addition  as we suspected if you try to optimize  only for the manual up votes and down  votes you don't do very well so you  don't get a high conversation quality  and you don't elicit much human reward  so even though this is kind of the  ground truth signal of what  conversations or what utterances the  humans enjoy it's not sufficient to be  able to train a good model because it's  just too sparse so when we instead use  these types of implicit signals like  sentiment we can do much better  we also looked at human evaluation  comparing each of the different models  so the  baseline offline rl methods that we  compare to  and as we suspected um  both in terms of the quality the votes  and the human reward the kl control  methods are are significantly  outperforming the rl baselines  but there is a caveat to these results  in terms of the original language model  that we used as our pre-trained prior  the rl methods do significantly  outperform it in terms of eliciting  human rewards  but they're actually not that good  in terms of beating the small in terms  of the conversation quality  so the original language model the  humans actually rate a slightly higher  quality which is somewhat ambiguous so  that could mean that our reward  functions are not sufficient to really  measure what is a high quality  conversation but i think there's  something else going on here um  in addition we don't have that much data  from this small human study but i think  there's something more profound which is  that  that model was learning to optimize  these rewards by modifying the  probability of outputting each and every  word so you're basically learning how to  maximize things like user satisfaction  at the word by word level and that's  actually a very hard learning problem so  what we did in some follow-up work is we  instead tried to learn the rewards at  the sentence level so we had a  hierarchical sequence of sequence model  where there's a sentence level embedding  that conditions a word level decoder  and by using continuous control to  modify that sentence level embedding we  can actually optimize for all of these  same rewards that i just discussed at  that work at that sentence level  and we see that this actually resolves  that issue so now what you're seeing is  not only does our rl model outperform  the rl baselines like reinforce um and  vhrl but what we see is that um  it actually outperforms both a  transformer language model and our  original language model that we base  this on so both in terms of human uh  ratings of quality as well as the amount  of time people spent talking to the bot  which we're taking is another implicit  signal of whether they enjoyed it in  this work  um  a fun thing to do is to look at you know  how well these models can optimize for  these different rewards so if we look at  our transformer baseline this is a  transformer trained on a pretty small  data set so it doesn't always have the  best performance um but we see that our  rl model can do things like optimize for  eliciting sentiment which we've seen i'm  in a sun room a lot and it's a great  feeling  it can optimize for asking questions so  um what do you do for fun we've seen  this  it can minimize repetition which is  important to maintaining conversation  quality as we saw and it can do this  more effectively again at the sentence  level than the word level so in this  conversation you know the user says how  are you i'm a reservoir engineer cool i  am still in college today is my first  day of vacation it's nice  it can also optimize the semantic  similarities so staying on topic with  the user so in this conversation the  user mentions ed sheeran the bot says  what genre is it and then the bot asks  for any recommendations so it's staying  on topic with what the user is saying  and finally and i think most importantly  in this work we also discuss the idea of  minimizing the toxicity of the model so  whenever you're training these dialogue  models on large-scale internet data sets  we know that they can exhibit biases  they can be exhibit racist and sexist  language  so what we did was we trained a detector  a classifier that says is this language  toxic and then what we can do is use  that as a reward function for our rl  model to try to minimize that toxicity  level and we see that our model is able  to do this pretty effectively and we  have  some results of that in the paper i  think this is actually really important  to emphasize why you might want to do rl  on top of language models right there  might be some non-differentiable metrics  that are really important for you to  minimize like toxicity that rl gives you  the ability to do that so whatever  metric you can decide should be a  quality of your text you can use these  rl methods to optimize for that  all right so in conclusion this is the  first work to propose kale control from  a pre-trained prior as a way to handle  offline or batch rl but the key thing  here is that we're using reinforcement  learning to to improve based on human  feedback and we showed that learning  from implicit signals from a human like  sentiment scales better than learning  from manual button presses  and as i mentioned in the last slide and  the other thing to note is that rl  allows you to optimize for any arbitrary  metric you can code up so if you want to  do something like reduce toxicity this  can be a really useful tool  all right so in conclusion social  reinforcement learning can improve  coordination with other agents human ai  interaction as well as fundamental  issues like learning and generalization  so i'll stop here and take any questions  thank you  all right so um  yeah unfortunately we can't take  questions but uh we hope you enjoyed her  talk  and  um i was making a joke that like uh yeah  you know usually like when people say  actually it's not a question but more  comment at least you can do that if you  want  so  uh but well if you we can also save time  for the run table yeah i think natasha  talk brings up a lot of issues that will  definitely be
 

